{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011erCGhRhTASQ2DiH57tb3P","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform scoring scale 1-5 soundness excitement overall\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Review Form\",\"url\":\"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform\"}]\n\nI found the ACL Rolling Review form. Here are the key scoring scales:\n\n**Soundness (1-5 scale):**\n1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community. 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type. 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential. 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n**Excitement (1-5 scale):**\n5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference. 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n**Overall Recommendation:**\nThe form asks if the paper should be accepted to an *ACL conference. If you recommend conference, Findings and or even award consideration, you can still suggest minor revisions (e.g. typos, non-core missing refs, etc.).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"1e7aa11f-5efa-4b48-9d1c-a89a7f6ca887","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:04:14.253Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"site:aclrollingreview.org reviewform scoring scale 1-5 soundness excitement overall","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01PtCvbQkcJJU2xgKVW7potr","content":[{"title":"Review Form","url":"http://aclrollingreview.org/reviewform"}]},"I found the ACL Rolling Review form. Here are the key scoring scales:\n\n**Soundness (1-5 scale):**\n1 = Not Exciting: this paper does not resonate with me, and I don't think it would with others in the *ACL community. 5 = Excellent: This study is one of the most thorough I have seen, given its type. 4 = Strong: This study provides sufficient support for all of its claims. Some extra experiments could be nice, but not essential. 3 = Acceptable: This study provides sufficient support for its main claims.\n\n**Excitement (1-5 scale):**\n5 = Highly Exciting: I would recommend this paper to others and/or attend its presentation in a conference. 4 = Exciting: I would mention this paper to others and/or make an effort to attend its presentation in a conference.\n\n**Overall Recommendation:**\nThe form asks if the paper should be accepted to an *ACL conference. If you recommend conference, Findings and or even award consideration, you can still suggest minor revisions (e.g. typos, non-core missing refs, etc.)."],"durationSeconds":4.263198649000006}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H3dCtFeQraQCemYC5RzjVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I now have enough information about:\n1. The paper content (8-page PDF about seed variance dominating error injection signal in low-data embodied planning SFT)\n2. The review context (experiment designs for 6 experiments)\n3. The venue (Insights from Negative Results in NLP @ EMNLP 2026 workshop) - uses ARR-style review process\n4. The ARR review form uses: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Overall Recommendation\n\nThe ARR scoring scales are:\n- **Soundness** (1-5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor\n\n\n- **Excitement** (1-5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Somewhat Exciting, 2=Not Exciting, 1=Not at all Exciting  \n- **Overall Recommendation**: 5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Accept/Findings, 3=Findings, 2.5=Resubmit next cycle+, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n\nNow I'm ready to start the review process.\n\nI'll assign the two reviewers in parallel: one to evaluate the methods and experimental design, the other to assess novelty and how this work fits into the existing literature. The paper tests a taxonomy-guided error injection approach on embodied planning tasks using ALFWorld, comparing vanilla, taxonomy-guided, and random error injection across three conditions on Qwen3-8B. The key finding is that single-seed results showing clear taxonomy benefits and performance plateaus disappear when running multiple seeds, suggesting seed variance is the dominant factor rather than the error injection signal itself.\n\nThe paper includes six experiments covering main results, training dynamics, scale ablation, WebShop replication, per-operator analysis, recovery prompting, and qualitative failure analysis. The core contributions are that agent SFT evaluation needs at least five seeds, and below 10K trajectories, error injection essentially acts as noise. This is being submitted to a workshop focused on negative results in NLP, which values this kind of finding that challenges initial assumptions.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1140,"cache_read_input_tokens":58268,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1140},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"dfcdc11e-331c-4537-a04c-a1255a4fd230"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H3dCtFeQraQCemYC5RzjVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I now have the venue guidelines. The Insights from Negative Results in NLP workshop uses the ARR review form with Soundness (1–5), Excitement (1–5), and Overall Recommendation scales. Now dispatching two reviewers in parallel."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1140,"cache_read_input_tokens":58268,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1140},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"b9ba70f5-8688-41a1-b0a4-41962f487020"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01H3dCtFeQraQCemYC5RzjVX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KcYgYNb9xM3Sohn2HQ93am","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. You must provide a thorough, independent assessment.\n\n## Venue\nInsights from Negative Results in NLP workshop @ EMNLP 2026. This workshop specifically values negative results that have implications for future research, highlight methodological issues, or point out bad practices. Uses the ARR review form:\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Somewhat Exciting, 2=Not Exciting, 1=Not at all Exciting\n- Overall Recommendation: 5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Accept/Findings, 3=Findings, 2.5=Resubmit next cycle+, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Seed Variance Dominates Error Injection Signal in Low-Data Embodied Planning SFT\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (TaxEI) — which showed large gains in tool-use domains (PALADIN on ToolBench) — transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). A 4-operator taxonomy (P1: step_order_swap, P2: sub_goal_deletion, C1: context_truncation, C4: observation_masking) is used to inject errors into 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful trajectories, producing 4,671 injected variants. Three SFT conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (9,029 clean+taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (9,029 clean+unlabeled injections). Base model: Qwen3-8B, 3 epochs, 3 seeds (42, 0, 123), 47 ALFWorld test tasks.\n\n**Key claim**: All initial single-seed (seed=42) findings (variance gradient, threshold-plateau dose-response, ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty) dissolve under multi-seed evaluation. Two contributions: (1) ≥5 seeds needed for agent SFT evaluation, (2) below ~10K trajectories, error injection signal is indistinguishable from noise.\n\n### Six experiments:\n1. **Main Results (Table 1)**: TaxEI 56.7%±8.6pp, Vanilla 65.3%±3.3pp, gap −8.6pp (p=0.32). Only 3 seeds used, extreme variance.\n2. **Training Dynamics (Table 2)**: Clean-injected loss gap for TaxEI is 0.02 at epoch 3; cross-seed SD (0.03–0.05) exceeds this gap.\n3. **Scale Ablation (Table 3)**: At 25K, gap shrinks to −2.0pp (p=0.35); at 50K, −1.0pp (p=0.52). Variance ratio 2.6× → 1.24×.\n4. **WebShop replication (Tables 4–5)**: TaxEI vs Vanilla −2.2pp (p=0.55). Null replicates; but ALFWorld-specific variance patterns don't transfer.\n5. **Per-Operator (Table 6)**: C4's seed=42 advantage +9.1pp reverses at other seeds; 3-seed mean = 0.0pp vs Vanilla.\n6. **Recovery Prompting (Table 7)**: Few-shot at inference yields ~2pp NS gain for all base models equally.\n7. **Qualitative Failure Analysis (Tables 8–9)**: Failure mode distributions TaxEI vs Vanilla: χ² p=0.91. Zero taxonomy operators produce elevated corresponding errors.\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (what the full experimental plan envisioned):\nAll 6 planned experiments appear covered in the paper:\n- Training dynamics analysis (covered in §3.2)\n- WebShop replication (§3.4)\n- Qualitative failure analysis (§3.7)\n- Scale ablation to 25K–50K (§3.3)\n- Recovery prompting (§3.6)\n- Per-operator multi-seed deep dive (§3.5)\n\nThe experimental plan notes that in the expected scenario, all original findings are seed=42 artifacts. The plan suggests the paper should reframe from \"error injection hurts\" to \"error injection is indistinguishable from noise in low-data embodied planning.\" The paper has indeed adopted this framing.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nAssess the following:\n\n### Methodological Soundness\n1. Is the experimental design valid for the claims made?\n2. Are 3 seeds sufficient to claim that single-seed conclusions are unreliable, or is 3→5 itself under-powered?\n3. Is the statistical testing appropriate (paired t-tests with n=3)?\n4. Is the comparison to PALADIN fair (different environment, different model, different data scale)?\n5. Are the hyperparameter choices justified? Is 3 epochs appropriate?\n6. Is using 47 test tasks sufficient for reliable evaluation?\n\n### Experiment Quality\n1. Are the tables and results clearly presented?\n2. Is the scale ablation (25K/50K) well-designed? How were additional trajectories generated?\n3. Is the WebShop replication a valid cross-environment test?\n4. Is the training dynamics analysis (loss decomposition) informative or trivially expected?\n5. Is the qualitative failure analysis (25 TaxEI + 20 Vanilla cases) sufficient sample size?\n6. Does the recovery prompting experiment add meaningful information?\n\n### Missing Experiments / Weaknesses\n1. What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n2. Are there confounds not addressed?\n3. Does the paper adequately address the data size mismatch between Vanilla (4,358) and TaxEI/Random (9,029)?\n\n### Strengths\nList the top 3–5 strengths of the methods and experiments.\n\n### Weaknesses\nList the top 3–5 weaknesses.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Paragraph-level detailed critique organized by section]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, formatting, presentation issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall Recommendation: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1140,"cache_read_input_tokens":58268,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1140},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"3b81c0b3-8c05-4893-a441-ba246246a5f6"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a73cbfd36a86421f5","tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcYgYNb9xM3Sohn2HQ93am","description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. You must provide a thorough, independent assessment.\n\n## Venue\nInsights from Negative Results in NLP workshop @ EMNLP 2026. This workshop specifically values negative results that have implications for future research, highlight methodological issues, or point out bad practices. Uses the ARR review form:\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Somewhat Exciting, 2=Not Exciting, 1=Not at all Exciting\n- Overall Recommendation: 5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Accept/Findings, 3=Findings, 2.5=Resubmit next cycle+, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Seed Variance Dominates Error Injection Signal in Low-Data Embodied Planning SFT\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (TaxEI) — which showed large gains in tool-use domains (PALADIN on ToolBench) — transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). A 4-operator taxonomy (P1: step_order_swap, P2: sub_goal_deletion, C1: context_truncation, C4: observation_masking) is used to inject errors into 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful trajectories, producing 4,671 injected variants. Three SFT conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (9,029 clean+taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (9,029 clean+unlabeled injections). Base model: Qwen3-8B, 3 epochs, 3 seeds (42, 0, 123), 47 ALFWorld test tasks.\n\n**Key claim**: All initial single-seed (seed=42) findings (variance gradient, threshold-plateau dose-response, ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty) dissolve under multi-seed evaluation. Two contributions: (1) ≥5 seeds needed for agent SFT evaluation, (2) below ~10K trajectories, error injection signal is indistinguishable from noise.\n\n### Six experiments:\n1. **Main Results (Table 1)**: TaxEI 56.7%±8.6pp, Vanilla 65.3%±3.3pp, gap −8.6pp (p=0.32). Only 3 seeds used, extreme variance.\n2. **Training Dynamics (Table 2)**: Clean-injected loss gap for TaxEI is 0.02 at epoch 3; cross-seed SD (0.03–0.05) exceeds this gap.\n3. **Scale Ablation (Table 3)**: At 25K, gap shrinks to −2.0pp (p=0.35); at 50K, −1.0pp (p=0.52). Variance ratio 2.6× → 1.24×.\n4. **WebShop replication (Tables 4–5)**: TaxEI vs Vanilla −2.2pp (p=0.55). Null replicates; but ALFWorld-specific variance patterns don't transfer.\n5. **Per-Operator (Table 6)**: C4's seed=42 advantage +9.1pp reverses at other seeds; 3-seed mean = 0.0pp vs Vanilla.\n6. **Recovery Prompting (Table 7)**: Few-shot at inference yields ~2pp NS gain for all base models equally.\n7. **Qualitative Failure Analysis (Tables 8–9)**: Failure mode distributions TaxEI vs Vanilla: χ² p=0.91. Zero taxonomy operators produce elevated corresponding errors.\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (what the full experimental plan envisioned):\nAll 6 planned experiments appear covered in the paper:\n- Training dynamics analysis (covered in §3.2)\n- WebShop replication (§3.4)\n- Qualitative failure analysis (§3.7)\n- Scale ablation to 25K–50K (§3.3)\n- Recovery prompting (§3.6)\n- Per-operator multi-seed deep dive (§3.5)\n\nThe experimental plan notes that in the expected scenario, all original findings are seed=42 artifacts. The plan suggests the paper should reframe from \"error injection hurts\" to \"error injection is indistinguishable from noise in low-data embodied planning.\" The paper has indeed adopted this framing.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nAssess the following:\n\n### Methodological Soundness\n1. Is the experimental design valid for the claims made?\n2. Are 3 seeds sufficient to claim that single-seed conclusions are unreliable, or is 3→5 itself under-powered?\n3. Is the statistical testing appropriate (paired t-tests with n=3)?\n4. Is the comparison to PALADIN fair (different environment, different model, different data scale)?\n5. Are the hyperparameter choices justified? Is 3 epochs appropriate?\n6. Is using 47 test tasks sufficient for reliable evaluation?\n\n### Experiment Quality\n1. Are the tables and results clearly presented?\n2. Is the scale ablation (25K/50K) well-designed? How were additional trajectories generated?\n3. Is the WebShop replication a valid cross-environment test?\n4. Is the training dynamics analysis (loss decomposition) informative or trivially expected?\n5. Is the qualitative failure analysis (25 TaxEI + 20 Vanilla cases) sufficient sample size?\n6. Does the recovery prompting experiment add meaningful information?\n\n### Missing Experiments / Weaknesses\n1. What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n2. Are there confounds not addressed?\n3. Does the paper adequately address the data size mismatch between Vanilla (4,358) and TaxEI/Random (9,029)?\n\n### Strengths\nList the top 3–5 strengths of the methods and experiments.\n\n### Weaknesses\nList the top 3–5 weaknesses.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Paragraph-level detailed critique organized by section]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, formatting, presentation issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall Recommendation: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n","uuid":"9dc75f07-c0e9-484a-8015-8cf1e642acb7","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a blind academic peer review. Your focus areas are **Methods** and **Experiments**. You must provide a thorough, independent assessment.\n\n## Venue\nInsights from Negative Results in NLP workshop @ EMNLP 2026. This workshop specifically values negative results that have implications for future research, highlight methodological issues, or point out bad practices. Uses the ARR review form:\n- Soundness (1–5): 5=Excellent, 4=Strong, 3=Acceptable, 2=Lacking, 1=Poor\n- Excitement (1–5): 5=Highly Exciting, 4=Exciting, 3=Somewhat Exciting, 2=Not Exciting, 1=Not at all Exciting\n- Overall Recommendation: 5=Award, 4=Accept, 3.5=Accept/Findings, 3=Findings, 2.5=Resubmit next cycle+, 2=Resubmit next cycle, 1.5=Resubmit after next cycle, 1=Do not resubmit\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title**: \"Seed Variance Dominates Error Injection Signal in Low-Data Embodied Planning SFT\"\n\nThe paper tests whether taxonomy-guided error injection (TaxEI) — which showed large gains in tool-use domains (PALADIN on ToolBench) — transfers to embodied planning (ALFWorld). A 4-operator taxonomy (P1: step_order_swap, P2: sub_goal_deletion, C1: context_truncation, C4: observation_masking) is used to inject errors into 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful trajectories, producing 4,671 injected variants. Three SFT conditions: Vanilla (4,358 clean), TaxEI (9,029 clean+taxonomy-tagged injections), Random (9,029 clean+unlabeled injections). Base model: Qwen3-8B, 3 epochs, 3 seeds (42, 0, 123), 47 ALFWorld test tasks.\n\n**Key claim**: All initial single-seed (seed=42) findings (variance gradient, threshold-plateau dose-response, ~6.4pp taxonomy penalty) dissolve under multi-seed evaluation. Two contributions: (1) ≥5 seeds needed for agent SFT evaluation, (2) below ~10K trajectories, error injection signal is indistinguishable from noise.\n\n### Six experiments:\n1. **Main Results (Table 1)**: TaxEI 56.7%±8.6pp, Vanilla 65.3%±3.3pp, gap −8.6pp (p=0.32). Only 3 seeds used, extreme variance.\n2. **Training Dynamics (Table 2)**: Clean-injected loss gap for TaxEI is 0.02 at epoch 3; cross-seed SD (0.03–0.05) exceeds this gap.\n3. **Scale Ablation (Table 3)**: At 25K, gap shrinks to −2.0pp (p=0.35); at 50K, −1.0pp (p=0.52). Variance ratio 2.6× → 1.24×.\n4. **WebShop replication (Tables 4–5)**: TaxEI vs Vanilla −2.2pp (p=0.55). Null replicates; but ALFWorld-specific variance patterns don't transfer.\n5. **Per-Operator (Table 6)**: C4's seed=42 advantage +9.1pp reverses at other seeds; 3-seed mean = 0.0pp vs Vanilla.\n6. **Recovery Prompting (Table 7)**: Few-shot at inference yields ~2pp NS gain for all base models equally.\n7. **Qualitative Failure Analysis (Tables 8–9)**: Failure mode distributions TaxEI vs Vanilla: χ² p=0.91. Zero taxonomy operators produce elevated corresponding errors.\n\n### Experiment Design Reference (what the full experimental plan envisioned):\nAll 6 planned experiments appear covered in the paper:\n- Training dynamics analysis (covered in §3.2)\n- WebShop replication (§3.4)\n- Qualitative failure analysis (§3.7)\n- Scale ablation to 25K–50K (§3.3)\n- Recovery prompting (§3.6)\n- Per-operator multi-seed deep dive (§3.5)\n\nThe experimental plan notes that in the expected scenario, all original findings are seed=42 artifacts. The plan suggests the paper should reframe from \"error injection hurts\" to \"error injection is indistinguishable from noise in low-data embodied planning.\" The paper has indeed adopted this framing.\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nAssess the following:\n\n### Methodological Soundness\n1. Is the experimental design valid for the claims made?\n2. Are 3 seeds sufficient to claim that single-seed conclusions are unreliable, or is 3→5 itself under-powered?\n3. Is the statistical testing appropriate (paired t-tests with n=3)?\n4. Is the comparison to PALADIN fair (different environment, different model, different data scale)?\n5. Are the hyperparameter choices justified? Is 3 epochs appropriate?\n6. Is using 47 test tasks sufficient for reliable evaluation?\n\n### Experiment Quality\n1. Are the tables and results clearly presented?\n2. Is the scale ablation (25K/50K) well-designed? How were additional trajectories generated?\n3. Is the WebShop replication a valid cross-environment test?\n4. Is the training dynamics analysis (loss decomposition) informative or trivially expected?\n5. Is the qualitative failure analysis (25 TaxEI + 20 Vanilla cases) sufficient sample size?\n6. Does the recovery prompting experiment add meaningful information?\n\n### Missing Experiments / Weaknesses\n1. What experiments are missing that would strengthen the paper?\n2. Are there confounds not addressed?\n3. Does the paper adequately address the data size mismatch between Vanilla (4,358) and TaxEI/Random (9,029)?\n\n### Strengths\nList the top 3–5 strengths of the methods and experiments.\n\n### Weaknesses\nList the top 3–5 weaknesses.\n\n## Output Format\nProvide your review in this exact structure:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods + Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper]\n\n### Strengths\n- S1: ...\n- S2: ...\n- S3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Weaknesses\n- W1: ...\n- W2: ...\n- W3: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n- Q1: ...\n[etc.]\n\n### Detailed Comments\n[Paragraph-level detailed critique organized by section]\n\n### Missing Experiments\n[What would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, formatting, presentation issues]\n\n### Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Overall Recommendation: X/5\n- Confidence: X/5 (1=low, 5=expert)\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcYgYNb9xM3Sohn2HQ93am","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"8461e194-a2ab-4751-9dff-9052a3f5654f","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:05:12.305Z"}
