{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01JjJVVP2iCDr83Czu2djThp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GCLZjkv3ffXhzWv5hXMi3q","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__read_project_file","input":{"project":"tax_error_inject_agent","file":"literature_map.yaml","max_chars":0},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1447,"cache_read_input_tokens":15879,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1447,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"702a5175-94bd-4948-86f1-bddc47ebccad"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GCLZjkv3ffXhzWv5hXMi3q","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"path\\\": \\\"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/tax_error_inject_agent/literature_map.yaml\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"# Literature Map: Dose-Response / Threshold Effects in Data Augmentation\\\\n# Updated: 2026-05-15\\\\n\\\\nentries:\\\\n  # === Category: dose-response / threshold ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: rolnick2017robust\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Deep Learning is Robust to Massive Label Noise\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Rolnick, Veit, Belongie, Shavit\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2017\\\\n    venue: arXiv 1705.10694\\\\n    category: dose-response\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"DNNs maintain >90% accuracy on MNIST even when correct labels are diluted 100:1, as long as correct label remains most likely by a small margin.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Contrasts with our finding: image classification is robust to extreme noise, while embodied planning shows sharp degradation at 25%. Task complexity mediates noise tolerance.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: li2024early_stopping\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Early Stopping Against Label Noise Without Validation Data\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: ICLR 2024\\\\n    category: dose-response\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Identifies phase transition where DNNs shift from fitting clean to fitting mislabeled examples. Proposes Label Wave to detect this transition during training.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Supports existence of phase transition in learning dynamics. Our 10-25% threshold may correspond to such a transition point in the SFT regime.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dohmatob2025collapse\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Strong Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dohmatob, Feng, Subramonian, Kempe\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: ICLR 2025\\\\n    category: threshold\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Even 0.1% synthetic data can cause model collapse in supervised regression. Past interpolation threshold, larger models become more robust (double-descent).\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Demonstrates extreme sensitivity to data quality in certain regimes. Our 10-25% threshold is analogous—small quality shifts cause nonlinear degradation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: beyond_clean_data2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Beyond Clean Data: Exploring the Effects of Label Noise on Object Detection Performance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: ScienceDirect\\\\n    category: dose-response\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Systematically varies noise levels in training and validation for object detection, mapping convergence limits and degradation thresholds.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Methodologically similar—varies noise proportion to find degradation curves. Shows task-specific threshold behavior.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # === Category: error-injection / failure augmentation ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wang2024learning_from_failure\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Learning From Failure: Integrating Negative Examples when Fine-tuning Large Language Models as Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Wang, Li, Han, Zhang, Baldwin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: arXiv 2402.11651\\\\n    category: error-injection\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Failed trajectories provide valuable signal for LLM agents via contrastive learning. Negative examples improve math, QA, strategic tasks when properly structured.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Directly related—injects negative trajectories into agent SFT. Key difference: they use contrastive framing, not raw mixing. Our work shows raw mixing has narrow safe window.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: song2024eto\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Trial and Error: Exploration-Based Trajectory Optimization for LLM Agents\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Song, Yin, Yue, Huang, Li, Lin\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: ACL 2024\\\\n    category: error-injection\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Iteratively collects failure trajectories, uses DPO-style contrastive learning on success-failure pairs. +8% WebShop, +9.5% ScienceWorld vs SFT.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Uses failure trajectories via preference optimization (DPO), not direct SFT mixing. Avoids our threshold problem by never training directly on failures.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: paladin2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"PALADIN: Self-Correcting Language Model Agents to Cure Tool-Failure Cases\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Vuddanti, Shah et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: arXiv 2509.25238\\\\n    category: error-injection\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Trains on 50K+ recovery-annotated failure trajectories via LoRA. +13.6% recovery rate, +10.2% task success. Taxonomy of 7 error classes.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Most similar to our approach (taxonomy-guided error injection). Key difference: they pair failures with recovery demos; we inject standalone error trajectories.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: agentHER2025\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"AgentHER: Hindsight Experience Replay for LLM Agent Trajectory Relabeling\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: arXiv 2603.21357\\\\n    category: error-injection\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Converts failed trajectories to valid training data by relabeling—failure for task A becomes success for task B.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Avoids noise problem by redefining failure as success for different goals. Contrasts with our direct injection approach.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # === Category: data-mixing ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: dong2024composition\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"How Abilities in Large Language Models are Affected by Supervised Fine-tuning Data Composition\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Dong, Yuan et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: ACL 2024\\\\n    category: data-mixing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Data amount per domain matters more than ratio. Low-data mixing enables cross-domain transfer; high-data mixing causes ability conflicts.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Documents ability-conflict at high data amounts—analogous to our threshold. Ratio secondary to amount suggests our threshold may be absolute-count driven.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: ye2025mixing_laws\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Data Mixing Laws: Optimizing Data Mixtures by Predicting Language Modeling Performance\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Ye et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: ICLR 2025\\\\n    category: data-mixing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Discovers quantitative scaling laws L=f(r) for mixture proportions. Accurately predicts critical proportion avoiding catastrophic forgetting.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Their 'critical proportion for catastrophic forgetting' is conceptually identical to our threshold—both mark a phase transition in mixing.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: li2025mixing_optimization\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Data Mixing Optimization for Supervised Fine-Tuning of Large Language Models\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Li et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2025\\\\n    venue: ICML 2025\\\\n    category: data-mixing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"SFT mixing as optimization with scaling-law parameterization. Optimal weights depend on model size and data scale.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Optimal mixing is model-size dependent—our 7B model may have different threshold than larger models. Finding is scale-specific.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: seddik2024synthetic_bound\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"How Bad is Training on Synthetic Data? A Statistical Analysis of Language Model Collapse\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Seddik et al.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: arXiv 2404.05090\\\\n    category: data-mixing\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Theoretical upper bounds on low-quality data proportion before collapse. Real data proportion must remain high.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Theoretical grounding for hard thresholds in quality mixing. Our empirical 10-25% window is consistent with their predictions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # === Category: curriculum / quality threshold ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: curriculum_robust2024\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Robust Curriculum Learning: from Clean Label Detection to Noisy Label Self-Correction\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: OpenReview\\\\n    category: curriculum\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Networks learn simple patterns first, then overfit noisy labels. Loss-based curriculum selection is unstable; errors accumulate multiplicatively.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Explains WHY threshold exists: once noisy proportion exceeds implicit curriculum capacity, errors accumulate. Our 25% may exceed model's capacity.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: wkm2024alfworld\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Agent Planning with World Knowledge Model\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Various\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2024\\\\n    venue: NeurIPS 2024\\\\n    category: curriculum\\\\n    role: evaluation\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"World knowledge models reduce hallucinatory actions in ALFWorld. SFT on expert trajectories prone to overfitting.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"ALFWorld baseline showing SFT quality sensitivity. Hallucination increase from poor training data parallels our error-injection degradation.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  # === Category: training-dynamics / memorization ===\\\\n\\\\n  - id: arpit2017memorization\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"A Closer Look at Memorization in Deep Networks\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Arpit, Jastrzębski, Ballas, Krueger, Bengio, Kanwal, Maharaj, Fischer, Courville, Bengio, Lacoste-Julien\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2017\\\\n    venue: ICML 2017\\\\n    category: training-dynamics\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"DNNs first learn clean patterns (early learning phase), then memorize noise. Training loss/accuracy improve on noisy data but generalization degrades. Dataset-independent capacity concepts are insufficient.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Directly explains our '50% injection → lowest train loss but worst eval' paradox. Higher injection = more noisy samples to memorize, better train metrics but worse generalization.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: liu2020early_learning_reg\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"Early-Learning Regularization Prevents Memorization of Noisy Labels\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Liu, Niles-Weed, Razavian, Fernandez-Granda\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2020\\\\n    venue: NeurIPS 2020\\\\n    category: training-dynamics\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Formalizes early learning phenomenon: models predict true labels for mislabeled examples early in training, then memorize wrong labels. ELR regularization exploits this window.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Our error-injected samples may be correctly handled early in training but memorized later, degrading clean-data generalization. Explains why more epochs could worsen the gap.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: zhou2023lima\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Zhou, Liu, Xu, Iyer, Sun, Du, Lin, Geng, Zhang, Patel, Yu, Hinton\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: NeurIPS 2023\\\\n    category: training-dynamics\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Superficial Alignment Hypothesis: LLM knowledge/capability comes from pretraining; SFT only teaches output style/format. Limited SFT data suffices.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Explains why lower SFT loss ≠ better downstream capability. Error injection improves surface-level fitting (lower loss) but doesn't enhance—and may damage—pretrained planning capability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: luo2023catastrophic_forgetting\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"An Empirical Study of Catastrophic Forgetting in Large Language Models During Continual Fine-tuning\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Luo, Xu, Zheng, Zhao, Yang, Bian, Zheng\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2023\\\\n    venue: arXiv 2308.08747\\\\n    category: training-dynamics\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"SFT is off-policy learning prone to overfit training distribution and forget pretraining knowledge. Forgetting severity increases with divergence between fine-tuning and pretraining distributions.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Error injection increases distributional gap from pretraining data, accelerating catastrophic forgetting. 50% injection → maximal divergence → most forgetting of planning capability.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\\n  - id: longpre2020augmentation\\\\n    title: \\\\\\\"To Augment or Not to Augment? A Comparative Study on Text Augmentation Techniques for Low-Resource NLP\\\\\\\"\\\\n    authors: \\\\\\\"Longpre, Wang, De Melo\\\\\\\"\\\\n    year: 2020\\\\n    venue: ACL (Computational Linguistics)\\\\n    category: training-dynamics\\\\n    role: background\\\\n    key_finding: \\\\\\\"Text augmentation methods assume label-preservation and distribution-stationarity; violations degrade performance. Token-level augmentation surprisingly hurts on complex tasks.\\\\\\\"\\\\n    relevance: \\\\\\\"Error injection is non-label-preserving augmentation that shifts distribution. Explains why Random 10% (mild shift, preserves diversity) helps but higher ratios (major shift) hurt.\\\\\\\"\\\\n\\\", \\\"total_chars\\\": 11502, \\\"truncated\\\": false}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"cf80d8bc-7088-445d-9d05-bd8e3033454a","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:05:55.063Z"}
