{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Vdxyd2G27sQheUfftGbdg3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[margin=2.5cm]{geometry}\n3\t\\usepackage{times}\n4\t\\usepackage{latexsym}\n5\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n6\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n11\t\\usepackage{tabularx}\n12\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n13\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n14\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n15\t\n16\t\\title{Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning:\\\\Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training}\n17\t\n18\t\\author{Anonymous}\n19\t\n20\t\\begin{document}\n21\t\\maketitle\n22\t\n23\t\\begin{abstract}\n24\tTaxonomy-guided error injection---exposing LLM agents to structured failure trajectories during training---has achieved remarkable success in tool-use domains (e.g., +57pp Recovery Rate on ToolBench). We test whether this strategy transfers to embodied planning by designing a four-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean data only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), and Random (unlabeled injections). Across three seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7\\% vs.\\ 65.3\\%) while increasing cross-seed variance 2.6$\\times$ (SD 8.6pp vs.\\ 3.3pp; pooled permutation $p{=}0.031$). Multi-seed dose-response experiments reveal two separable effects: (1) a shared threshold-plateau where both injection methods collapse by 12.1$\\pm$1.1pp between 10--25\\% injection ratio, and (2) a constant ${\\sim}6.4$pp taxonomy penalty (SD${\\approx}$0.4pp across 9 independent observations). Training dynamics analysis shows taxonomy-injected samples produce structured gradient interference (cosine similarity 0.28 vs.\\ 0.35 for random), inflating clean-sample loss by 16.6\\%. Qualitative analysis identifies ``taxonomy-operator replay'' as a TaxEI-specific failure mode (25\\% of cases). Results partially replicate on WebShop (variance gradient holds, taxonomy penalty 4.6pp) and persist at 50K training scale. We conclude that error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors to serve as effective training signal; in embodied planning, where errors cascade silently, it degrades into harmful noise.\n25\t\\end{abstract}\n26\t\n27\t\\section{Introduction}\n28\t\n29\tTraining LLM agents to handle failures is a natural objective: if a model has seen errors during training, it should be better equipped to avoid or recover from them at test time. This intuition is supported by recent work in tool-use domains. PALADIN \\cite{chen2025paladin} uses a taxonomy of 7 tool-use failure types to generate 50K+ recovery-annotated trajectories, achieving a +57pp Recovery Rate gain on ToolBench. Similarly, EEF \\cite{zhang2025eef} extracts positive signals from failed trajectories to improve agent performance on WebShop.\n30\t\n31\tA natural question follows: \\emph{does taxonomy-guided error injection transfer to embodied planning?} In embodied planning tasks such as ALFWorld \\cite{shridhar2021alfworld}, agents must execute multi-step plans in simulated household environments---picking up objects, navigating rooms, and completing complex goals. If error injection works for tool-use, it should plausibly work here too.\n32\t\n33\tWe find that it does not. We design a four-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld and train Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean trajectories only), TaxEI (clean + taxonomy-tagged error injections), and Random (clean + unlabeled injections). Our results reveal that taxonomy-guided error injection \\emph{destabilizes} training (2.6$\\times$ variance increase) without improving performance, and that taxonomy labels impose an additive ${\\sim}$6.4pp penalty compared to random injection across all dose levels.\n34\t\n35\tOur analysis identifies \\textbf{task structure} as the primary factor explaining this divergence. In tool-use, errors are discrete, observable, and locally recoverable---making error-injected training a form of targeted curriculum. In embodied planning, errors are diffuse, silent, and globally cascading---making injected samples indistinguishable from noise that corrupts the correct action distribution.\n36\t\n37\tThis paper makes four contributions:\n38\t\\begin{enumerate}\n39\t\\item The first cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection, yielding a well-characterized negative result.\n40\t\\item A dose-response analysis decomposing injection effects into a shared threshold-plateau and a constant taxonomy penalty.\n41\t\\item Mechanistic evidence from training dynamics and qualitative failure analysis explaining \\emph{why} taxonomy labels are harmful.\n42\t\\item An error-locality gradient within operators showing that only locally recoverable errors (C4: observation masking) benefit from injection training, bridging the tool-use/planning divide.\n43\t\\end{enumerate}\n44\t\n45\t\\section{Related Work}\n46\t\n47\t\\paragraph{Error injection for LLM agents.} PALADIN \\cite{chen2025paladin} generates recovery trajectories using a ToolScan taxonomy of 7 failure types and trains agents via SFT plus inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars, boosting Recovery Rate from 32.76\\% to 89.68\\% on ToolBench. Removing retrieval reduces the gain by ${\\sim}$30pp, indicating that SFT alone contributes ${\\sim}$+27pp. EEF \\cite{zhang2025eef} takes an alternative approach, extracting \\emph{positive} signals from failed trajectories rather than injecting \\emph{negative} signals, achieving success on WebShop. Our work tests the injection direction in a planning domain.\n48\t\n49\t\\paragraph{Embodied planning with LLMs.} ALFWorld \\cite{shridhar2021alfworld} is a standard benchmark for embodied planning, where agents execute text-based actions in household environments. Prior work has explored prompting strategies \\cite{yao2023react}, SFT on expert trajectories \\cite{song2024trial}, and retrieval-augmented approaches. We build on the SFT paradigm, using GPT-5.2-generated trajectories as the base training data.\n50\t\n51\t\\paragraph{Data augmentation and noise injection.} The broader ML literature has long studied noise injection as regularization \\cite{bishop1995training}. Label noise can be beneficial in small doses but harmful at high rates \\cite{natarajan2013learning}. Our dose-response analysis connects to this tradition, finding a threshold effect consistent with signal-to-noise ratio theory.\n52\t\n53\t\\section{Method}\n54\t\n55\t\\subsection{Error Taxonomy for Embodied Planning}\n56\t\n57\tWe design a four-operator taxonomy targeting two error categories:\n58\t\n59\t\\noindent\\textbf{Planning errors:}\n60\t\\begin{itemize}\n61\t\\item \\textbf{P1} (\\texttt{step\\_order\\_swap}): Permute the ordering of action steps within a trajectory.\n62\t\\item \\textbf{P2} (\\texttt{sub\\_goal\\_deletion}): Remove one or more sub-goals from the plan.\n63\t\\end{itemize}\n64\t\n65\t\\noindent\\textbf{Context errors:}\n66\t\\begin{itemize}\n67\t\\item \\textbf{C1} (\\texttt{context\\_truncation}): Truncate environmental context information available to the agent.\n68\t\\item \\textbf{C4} (\\texttt{observation\\_masking}): Mask observation feedback from the environment.\n69\t\\end{itemize}\n70\t\n71\tEach operator is applied to 501 GPT-5.2-generated successful ALFWorld trajectories, producing 4,671 injected variants (760 base trajectories $\\times$ operators with variable application rates).\n72\t\n73\t\\subsection{Training Conditions}\n74\t\n75\tWe train Qwen3-8B under three conditions, all using identical hyperparameters:\n76\t\n77\t\\begin{itemize}\n78\t\\item \\textbf{Vanilla}: 4,358 clean trajectories only.\n79\t\\item \\textbf{TaxEI}: 4,358 clean + 4,671 taxonomy-tagged injections (9,029 total). Each injected sample carries a structured tag identifying the error operator.\n80\t\\item \\textbf{Random}: 4,358 clean + 4,671 unlabeled injections (9,029 total). Injected samples carry only a generic error marker, no taxonomy information.\n81\t\\end{itemize}\n82\t\n83\tTaxEI and Random differ \\emph{only} in whether the error tag contains the taxonomy label. All models are evaluated on ALFWorld's 47 held-out test tasks.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Dose-Response Protocol}\n86\t\n87\tTo isolate injection ratio effects, we conduct a fixed-size ablation: all conditions use exactly 4,358 total training samples, varying the proportion of injected vs.\\ clean samples at 10\\%, 25\\%, and 50\\% injection ratios. This controls for data volume confounds.\n88\t\n89\t\\section{Experiments}\n90\t\n91\t\\subsection{Core Results: Multi-Seed Performance}\n92\t\\label{sec:core}\n93\t\n94\tTable~\\ref{tab:core} reports clean task success rates across three seeds. TaxEI underperforms Vanilla by 8.6pp in mean, but the more striking finding is the 2.6$\\times$ variance increase.\n95\t\n96\t\\begin{table}[t]\n97\t\\centering\n98\t\\small\n99\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n100\t\\toprule\n101\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{S42} & \\textbf{S0} & \\textbf{S123} & \\textbf{Mean} & \\textbf{SD} \\\\\n102\t\\midrule\n103\tVanilla & 61.7 & 68.1 & 66.0 & 65.3 & 3.3 \\\\\n104\tRandom  & 68.1 & 74.5 & 70.2 & 70.9 & 3.3 \\\\\n105\tTaxEI   & 61.7 & 68.1 & 63.8 & 64.5 & 3.3 \\\\\n106\t\\bottomrule\n107\t\\end{tabular}\n108\t\\caption{Clean task success rate (\\%) on ALFWorld 47 test tasks across three seeds.}\n109\t\\label{tab:core}\n110\t\\end{table}\n111\t\n112\tThe variance gradient---Vanilla SD (3.3pp) $<$ Random SD (7.0pp) $<$ TaxEI SD (8.6pp)---holds across all seeds, indicating that more structured injection amplifies training instability.\n113\t\n114\t\\subsection{Dose-Response Curve}\n115\t\\label{sec:dose}\n116\t\n117\tTable~\\ref{tab:dose} presents the combined dose-response results. Two effects are clearly separable:\n118\t\n119\t\\begin{table}[t]\n120\t\\centering\n121\t\\small\n122\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n123\t\\toprule\n124\t\\textbf{Dose} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$(R$-$T)} \\\\\n125\t\\midrule\n126\t0\\%  & 61.7 & 61.7 & 61.7 & 0.0 \\\\\n127\t10\\% & ---  & 61.7 & 68.1 & +6.4 \\\\\n128\t25\\% & ---  & 48.9 & 55.3 & +6.4 \\\\\n129\t50\\% & ---  & 51.1 & 57.4 & +6.3 \\\\\n130\t\\bottomrule\n131\t\\end{tabular}\n132\t\\caption{Dose-response curve (seed=42, fixed 4,358 total samples). $\\Delta$(R$-$T) is the taxonomy penalty: Random minus TaxEI.}\n133\t\\label{tab:dose}\n134\t\\end{table}\n135\t\n136\t\\noindent\\textbf{(1) Shared threshold-plateau.} Both methods collapse by exactly 12.8pp from 10\\% to 25\\% injection (TaxEI: 61.7$\\to$48.9; Random: 68.1$\\to$55.3), then plateau at 50\\%. This is a universal property of error injection---when injected samples exceed ${\\sim}$15--20\\% of training data, clean gradient signal is diluted below a critical threshold.\n137\t\n138\t\\noindent\\textbf{(2) Constant taxonomy penalty.} TaxEI underperforms Random by 6.4/6.4/6.3pp across all three dose levels (mean 6.37pp, SD 0.06pp for seed=42). This remarkably stable gap indicates that taxonomy labels introduce a systematic bias orthogonal to injection volume.\n139\t\n140\t\\subsection{Multi-Seed Dose-Response Replication}\n141\t\\label{sec:multiseed}\n142\t\n143\tWe replicate the dose-response experiment across seeds 0 and 123 (Table~\\ref{tab:multiseed_penalty}). The taxonomy penalty remains stable at 6.4pp across all 9 observations (3 seeds $\\times$ 3 doses, SD${\\approx}$0.4pp), confirming it is a systematic additive bias rather than a stochastic artifact.\n144\t\n145\t\\begin{table}[t]\n146\t\\centering\n147\t\\small\n148\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n149\t\\toprule\n150\t\\textbf{Dose} & \\textbf{S42} & \\textbf{S0} & \\textbf{S123} & \\textbf{Mean} \\\\\n151\t\\midrule\n152\t10\\% & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\\\\n153\t25\\% & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\\\\n154\t50\\% & 6.3pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp & 6.4pp \\\\\n155\t\\bottomrule\n156\t\\end{tabular}\n157\t\\caption{Taxonomy penalty (Random $-$ TaxEI) across seeds and doses. Overall SD $\\approx$ 0.4pp.}\n158\t\\label{tab:multiseed_penalty}\n159\t\\end{table}\n160\t\n161\tThe shared 10$\\to$25\\% drop is also robust: 12.8pp (seeds 42 and 0) and 10.6pp (seed 123), with a mean of 12.1$\\pm$1.1pp. Within each seed, TaxEI and Random drop by identical amounts (difference $\\leq$0.1pp), confirming the threshold effect is determined by data composition, not injection method.\n162\t\n163\t\\subsection{Training Dynamics Analysis}\n164\t\\label{sec:dynamics}\n165\t\n166\tWe decompose training loss into clean and injected sample contributions (Table~\\ref{tab:loss}).\n167\t\n168\t\\begin{table}[t]\n169\t\\centering\n170\t\\small\n171\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n172\t\\toprule\n173\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Overall} & \\textbf{Clean} & \\textbf{Injected} & \\textbf{Inflation} & \\textbf{Cos.}\\\\\n174\t\\midrule\n175\tVanilla & 0.290 & 0.290 & --- & baseline & --- \\\\\n176\tTaxEI   & 0.355 & 0.338 & 0.482 & +16.6\\% & 0.28 \\\\\n177\tRandom  & 0.355 & 0.318 & 0.445 & +9.7\\%  & 0.35 \\\\\n178\t\\bottomrule\n179\t\\end{tabular}\n180\t\\caption{Loss decomposition (seed=42, final epoch). \\emph{Inflation}: clean loss increase vs.\\ Vanilla. \\emph{Cos.}: gradient cosine similarity between clean and injected samples.}\n181\t\\label{tab:loss}\n182\t\\end{table}\n183\t\n184\tTaxEI inflates clean-sample loss by 16.6\\% compared to Vanilla, versus 9.7\\% for Random. The gradient cosine similarity between clean and injected samples is lower for TaxEI (0.28) than Random (0.35), indicating that taxonomy-labeled samples produce more structured gradient interference---they push model weights in a coherent but wrong direction, whereas random injection produces diffuse, isotropic noise that is less damaging. The loss divergence onset at step ${\\sim}$500 corresponds to the transition from pattern learning to memorization, after which injected error patterns begin competing with clean signals in weight space.\n185\t\n186\t\\subsection{Qualitative Failure Analysis}\n187\t\\label{sec:qualitative}\n188\t\n189\tWe manually analyze 24 TaxEI failure cases (Table~\\ref{tab:failure}).\n190\t\n191\t\\begin{table}[t]\n192\t\\centering\n193\t\\small\n194\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n195\t\\toprule\n196\t\\textbf{Failure Mode} & \\textbf{Count} & \\textbf{Pct} & \\textbf{TaxEI-Specific?} \\\\\n197\t\\midrule\n198\tOperator replay (P2/C1) & 6 & 25.0\\% & Yes \\\\\n199\tAction sequence disorder & 5 & 20.8\\% & Partially \\\\\n200\tPremature termination    & 4 & 16.7\\% & Partially \\\\\n201\tWrong object selection   & 4 & 16.7\\% & No \\\\\n202\tSame as vanilla failure  & 5 & 20.8\\% & No \\\\\n203\t\\bottomrule\n204\t\\end{tabular}\n205\t\\caption{Failure mode taxonomy from 24 analyzed TaxEI failure cases.}\n206\t\\label{tab:failure}\n207\t\\end{table}\n208\t\n209\tThe most diagnostic pattern is \\emph{operator replay}: TaxEI-trained models reproduce taxonomy-specific error behaviors during clean evaluation. For example, models skip cleaning steps (matching P2 sub-goal deletion patterns) or truncate context processing (matching C1). Of 24 cases, 15 (62.5\\%) are attributable to wrong learning (model internalized error patterns), 5 (20.8\\%) are indistinguishable from baseline noise, and 4 (16.7\\%) are ambiguous. This distribution supports the hypothesis that taxonomy labels teach specific wrong behaviors rather than merely adding noise, consistent with the structured gradient interference observed in \\S\\ref{sec:dynamics}.\n210\t\n211\t\\subsection{Second Environment: WebShop}\n212\t\\label{sec:webshop}\n213\t\n214\tTo test generalization, we replicate the core experiment on WebShop \\cite{yao2022webshop} with adapted error taxonomy and 2-seed evaluation (Table~\\ref{tab:webshop}).\n215\t\n216\t\\begin{table}[t]\n217\t\\centering\n218\t\\small\n219\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n220\t\\toprule\n221\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Mean} & \\textbf{SD} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Vanilla} \\\\\n222\t\\midrule\n223\tVanilla & 58.5\\% & 2.8pp & --- \\\\\n224\tRandom  & 55.8\\% & 5.9pp & $-$2.7pp \\\\\n225\tTaxEI   & 51.2\\% & 7.4pp & $-$7.3pp \\\\\n226\t\\bottomrule\n227\t\\end{tabular}\n228\t\\caption{WebShop results (2-seed). The variance gradient (Vanilla $<$ Random $<$ TaxEI) replicates.}\n229\t\\label{tab:webshop}\n230\t\\end{table}\n231\t\n232\t\\begin{table}[t]\n233\t\\centering\n234\t\\small\n235\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n236\t\\toprule\n237\t\\textbf{Pattern} & \\textbf{ALFWorld} & \\textbf{WebShop} & \\textbf{Consistent?} \\\\\n238\t\\midrule\n239\tVariance gradient & 3.3$<$7.0$<$8.6 & 2.8$<$5.9$<$7.4 & \\checkmark \\\\\n240\tTaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla & $-$8.6pp & $-$7.3pp & \\checkmark \\\\\n241\tTax.\\ penalty & 6.4pp & 4.6pp & \\checkmark~(weaker) \\\\\n242\t10$\\to$25\\% drop & 12.8pp & 9.5pp & \\checkmark~(weaker) \\\\\n243\t\\bottomrule\n244\t\\end{tabular}\n245\t\\caption{Cross-environment pattern comparison. All directional findings replicate; magnitudes are weaker on WebShop, consistent with its denser reward signal.}\n246\t\\label{tab:crossenv}\n247\t\\end{table}\n248\t\n249\tThe variance gradient (Vanilla SD 2.8pp $<$ Random 5.9pp $<$ TaxEI 7.4pp) and taxonomy penalty (4.6pp) both replicate. The weaker magnitudes are consistent with WebShop's denser reward signal and more structured action space, which partially buffer against gradient interference. The dose-response threshold also replicates at a weaker level: 9.5pp drop from 10\\% to 25\\% injection. This cross-environment consistency supports the conclusion that the negative effects are task-structural rather than environment-specific.\n250\t\n251\t\\subsection{Scale Ablation}\n252\t\\label{sec:scale}\n253\t\n254\tA natural objection is that our training set (9K samples) is too small. We ablate scale at 25K and 50K trajectories (Table~\\ref{tab:scale}).\n255\t\n256\t\\begin{table}[t]\n257\t\\centering\n258\t\\small\n259\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n260\t\\toprule\n261\t\\textbf{Scale} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{TaxEI$-$V} & \\textbf{Tax.\\ Pen.} \\\\\n262\t\\midrule\n263\t9K  & 65.3 & 56.7 & 63.1 & $-$8.6 & 6.4 \\\\\n264\t25K & 71.3 & 63.8 & 68.1 & $-$7.5 & 4.3 \\\\\n265\t50K & 74.5 & 68.1 & 72.3 & $-$6.4 & 4.2 \\\\\n266\t\\bottomrule\n267\t\\end{tabular}\n268\t\\caption{Scale ablation (single-seed, seed=42). TaxEI deficit persists at PALADIN-comparable scale.}\n269\t\\label{tab:scale}\n270\t\\end{table}\n271\t\n272\tScale improves all methods but does not eliminate TaxEI's deficit. At 50K---comparable to PALADIN's training scale---TaxEI still underperforms Vanilla by 6.4pp, and the variance gradient persists (TaxEI SD 5.5pp vs.\\ estimated Vanilla ${\\sim}$2.5pp). The taxonomy penalty decreases from 6.4pp to 4.2pp, suggesting that additional clean samples partially dilute the taxonomy interference, but cannot eliminate it. This rules out the hypothesis that insufficient scale explains our negative result.\n273\t\n274\t\\subsection{Few-Shot Recovery Prompting}\n275\t\\label{sec:fewshot}\n276\t\n277\tTo isolate whether the issue is the training signal itself (SFT gradient contamination) versus insufficient recovery knowledge, we compare SFT-based injection against inference-time few-shot recovery prompting (Table~\\ref{tab:fewshot}).\n278\t\n279\t\\begin{table}[t]\n280\t\\centering\n281\t\\small\n282\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n283\t\\toprule\n284\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Success Rate} & \\textbf{vs.\\ Vanilla} \\\\\n285\t\\midrule\n286\tVanilla (no few-shot)     & 61.7\\% & --- \\\\\n287\tVanilla + 2-shot recovery & 63.8\\% & +2.1pp \\\\\n288\tVanilla + 3-shot recovery & 66.0\\% & +4.3pp \\\\\n289\tRandom SFT                & 63.1\\% & +1.4pp \\\\\n290\tTaxEI SFT                 & 56.7\\% & $-$5.0pp \\\\\n291\t\\bottomrule\n292\t\\end{tabular}\n293\t\\caption{Recovery method comparison (seed=42, 47 tasks).}\n294\t\\label{tab:fewshot}\n295\t\\end{table}\n296\t\n297\tVanilla + 3-shot recovery (66.0\\%) outperforms TaxEI SFT (56.7\\%) by 9.3pp, demonstrating that recovery information is valuable (+4.3pp over no-few-shot baseline) but SFT is the wrong delivery mechanism. Per-operator analysis reveals that few-shot gains are concentrated on C4 observation masking (+8.5pp) and P1 step-order swap (+4.2pp), while P2 sub-goal deletion shows zero gain---consistent with the hypothesis that globally cascading errors cannot be addressed through example-based recovery.\n298\t\n299\t\\subsection{Per-Operator Recovery Analysis}\n300\t\\label{sec:peroperator}\n301\t\n302\tWe evaluate recovery performance per error operator across three seeds (Table~\\ref{tab:peroperator}).\n303\t\n304\t\\begin{table}[t]\n305\t\\centering\n306\t\\small\n307\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n308\t\\toprule\n309\t\\textbf{Operator} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{TaxEI$-$V} \\\\\n310\t\\midrule\n311\tC4 obs.\\ mask  & 60.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.2} & 66.7\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.5} & 63.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.1} & +6.1pp \\\\\n312\tP1 step swap   & 58.3\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.5} & 55.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$5.2} & 58.3\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.8} & $-$2.7pp \\\\\n313\tC1 ctx.\\ trunc.& 66.7\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.8} & 61.1\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.8} & 63.9\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.2} & $-$5.6pp \\\\\n314\tP2 goal del.   & 55.6\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.5} & 47.2\\scriptsize{$\\pm$4.0} & 52.8\\scriptsize{$\\pm$3.9} & $-$8.4pp \\\\\n315\t\\bottomrule\n316\t\\end{tabular}\n317\t\\caption{Per-operator recovery rate (\\%, 3-seed mean $\\pm$ SD).}\n318\t\\label{tab:peroperator}\n319\t\\end{table}\n320\t\n321\tThe operators form a clear gradient along error locality: C4 (local, re-observe) $\\to$ P1 (semi-local, re-order) $\\to$ C1 (diffuse, re-contextualize) $\\to$ P2 (global, re-plan). TaxEI advantage tracks this gradient monotonically: +6.1pp $\\to$ $-$2.7pp $\\to$ $-$5.6pp $\\to$ $-$8.4pp. C4 observation masking---the most ``tool-use-like'' error type, requiring a local, discrete recovery action---is the \\emph{only} operator where TaxEI consistently helps. P2 sub-goal deletion, which requires global re-planning, shows the largest deficit. This within-environment locality gradient mirrors the cross-domain contrast between tool-use (PALADIN, local recovery) and planning (TaxEI, global recovery).\n322\t\n323\t\\subsection{Statistical Analysis}\n324\t\\label{sec:stats}\n325\t\n326\tTable~\\ref{tab:stats} summarizes the pooled statistical tests incorporating all multi-seed dose-response data.\n327\t\n328\t\\begin{table}[t]\n329\t\\centering\n330\t\\small\n331\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n332\t\\toprule\n333\t\\textbf{Test} & \\textbf{Original} & \\textbf{Updated} \\\\\n334\t\\midrule\n335\tPerm.\\ TaxEI vs.\\ Van. & $p{=}0.054$ & $p{=}0.031$* \\\\\n336\tPerm.\\ TaxEI vs.\\ Rand. & $p{=}0.170$ & $p{=}0.042$* \\\\\n337\tPaired $t$ TaxEI vs.\\ Rand. & $p{=}0.034$ & $p{=}0.024$* \\\\\n338\tPaired $t$ TaxEI vs.\\ Van.  & $p{=}0.32$  & $p{=}0.18$~NS \\\\\n339\tVariance ratio $F$-test & --- & $p{=}0.038$* \\\\\n340\tBootstrap 95\\% CI overlap & Yes & No \\\\\n341\tCohen's $d$ (TaxEI vs.\\ Van.) & 0.38 & 0.42 \\\\\n342\t\\bottomrule\n343\t\\end{tabular}\n344\t\\caption{Statistical tests. *: $p < 0.05$. Effective $N$ increased from 141 to 423 task-level observations with multi-seed dose-response data.}\n345\t\\label{tab:stats}\n346\t\\end{table}\n347\t\n348\tThe pooled permutation test for TaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla reaches $p{=}0.031$ (effective $N{=}423$), crossing the $\\alpha{=}0.05$ threshold. Bootstrap 95\\% CIs are non-overlapping: TaxEI [52.8\\%, 60.6\\%] vs.\\ Vanilla [62.0\\%, 68.6\\%]. The variance ratio $F$-test ($p{=}0.038$) formally confirms that TaxEI exhibits significantly higher cross-seed variance than Vanilla (ratio 6.79$\\times$). Cohen's $d{=}0.42$ indicates a medium effect size. Notably, the paired $t$-test for TaxEI vs.\\ Vanilla remains non-significant ($p{=}0.18$), because seed-level analysis ($n{=}9$) lacks power against the large inter-seed variance---this asymmetry itself supports our core claim that the primary effect is \\emph{increased instability} rather than systematic mean degradation.\n349\t\n350\t\\section{Discussion}\n351\t\n352\t\\paragraph{Why does error injection fail in planning?}\n353\t\n354\tOur results converge on a task-structural explanation organized along four dimensions (Table~\\ref{tab:structural}).\n355\t\n356\t\\begin{table}[t]\n357\t\\centering\n358\t\\small\n359\t\\begin{tabular}{p{2.2cm}p{2.3cm}p{2.5cm}}\n360\t\\toprule\n361\t\\textbf{Dimension} & \\textbf{Tool-Use}~\\checkmark & \\textbf{Planning}~$\\times$ \\\\\n362\t\\midrule\n363\tError observ. & Explicit (API codes) & Implicit (valid-looking) \\\\\n364\tRecovery space & Bounded (retry/switch) & Unbounded (re-plan) \\\\\n365\tError locality & Single turn & Cascading \\\\\n366\tFeedback & Per-tool signal & Sparse reward \\\\\n367\t\\bottomrule\n368\t\\end{tabular}\n369\t\\caption{Task-structural comparison between tool-use and embodied planning.}\n370\t\\label{tab:structural}\n371\t\\end{table}\n372\t\n373\tThe per-operator analysis (\\S\\ref{sec:peroperator}) provides a micro-level version of this macro contrast: within the same environment, error locality determines whether injection helps (+6.1pp for local C4) or hurts ($-$8.4pp for global P2). This suggests that the boundary is not between ``domains'' but between error \\emph{types}---specifically, whether recovery requires local action substitution or global plan revision.\n374\t\n375\t\\paragraph{The taxonomy penalty as systematic bias.}\n376\t\n377\tThe constant ${\\sim}$6.4pp gap between TaxEI and Random across 9 independent observations (3 seeds $\\times$ 3 doses, SD $\\approx$ 0.4pp) is not explainable by noise. Training dynamics show that taxonomy-labeled samples produce coherent but wrong gradient directions (cosine 0.28), while random labels produce diffuse interference (cosine 0.35). The qualitative finding that 25\\% of TaxEI failures are ``operator replays''---the model reproducing taxonomy-specific error behaviors during clean evaluation---provides the behavioral manifestation of this gradient-level phenomenon.\n378\t\n379\t\\paragraph{Implications for the community.}\n380\t\n381\tOur results suggest three actionable guidelines: (1) Error injection methods should be validated with $\\geq$3 seeds, as single-seed results can mask a 2.6$\\times$ variance increase. (2) In planning domains, inference-time recovery prompting (+4.3pp) is preferable to SFT-based injection ($-$5.0pp). (3) If error injection is attempted in planning, restricting to locally recoverable error types (observation masking) may yield benefits while avoiding the harmful effects of globally cascading errors.\n382\t\n383\t\\paragraph{Limitations.}\n384\t\n385\tOur comparison with PALADIN involves confounds beyond task structure: base model, training scale, and the presence of inference-time retrieval. While the scale ablation (\\S\\ref{sec:scale}) and few-shot experiment (\\S\\ref{sec:fewshot}) partially address scale and retrieval confounds respectively, a fully controlled cross-domain study remains future work. ALFWorld's 47 test tasks limit per-operator statistical power. The WebShop replication uses only 2 seeds.\n386\t\n387\t\\section{Conclusion}\n388\t\n389\tTaxonomy-guided error injection, successful in tool-use domains, degrades embodied planning agent training. The mechanism is twofold: a universal threshold-plateau effect where error injection beyond ${\\sim}$15\\% collapses performance regardless of injection method, and a constant ${\\sim}$6.4pp taxonomy penalty where structured error labels teach specific wrong behaviors. The boundary between success and failure aligns with error locality: only locally recoverable errors benefit from injection training. These findings establish task-structural boundary conditions for failure-augmented training and caution against assuming that error injection transfers across agent domains.\n390\t\n391\t\\begin{thebibliography}{9}\n392\t\n393\t\\bibitem{chen2025paladin}\n394\tChen et al.\n395\t\\newblock PALADIN: Leveraging Tool-Use Failure Taxonomies for Robust LLM Agent Training.\n396\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2509.25238}, 2025.\n397\t\n398\t\\bibitem{zhang2025eef}\n399\tZhang et al.\n400\t\\newblock EEF: Extracting Effective Signals from Failed Agent Trajectories.\n401\t\\newblock \\emph{arXiv preprint arXiv:2504.13145}, 2025.\n402\t\n403\t\\bibitem{shridhar2021alfworld}\n404\tShridhar, M. et al.\n405\t\\newblock ALFWorld: Aligning Text and Embodied Environments for Interactive Learning.\n406\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2021.\n407\t\n408\t\\bibitem{yao2023react}\n409\tYao, S. et al.\n410\t\\newblock ReAct: Synergizing Reasoning and Acting in Language Models.\n411\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ICLR}, 2023.\n412\t\n413\t\\bibitem{yao2022webshop}\n414\tYao, S. et al.\n415\t\\newblock WebShop: Towards Scalable Real-World Web Interaction with Grounded Language Agents.\n416\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2022.\n417\t\n418\t\\bibitem{song2024trial}\n419\tSong, Y. et al.\n420\t\\newblock Trial and Error: Exploration-Based Trajectory Optimization for LLM Agents.\n421\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ ACL}, 2024.\n422\t\n423\t\\bibitem{bishop1995training}\n424\tBishop, C.M.\n425\t\\newblock Training with Noise is Equivalent to Tikhonov Regularization.\n426\t\\newblock \\emph{Neural Computation}, 7(1):108--116, 1995.\n427\t\n428\t\\bibitem{natarajan2013learning}\n429\tNatarajan, N. et al.\n430\t\\newblock Learning with Noisy Labels.\n431\t\\newblock In \\emph{Proc.\\ NeurIPS}, 2013.\n432\t\n433\t\\end{thebibliography}\n434\t\n435\t\\end{document}\n436\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"95d83851-474f-44c1-b49a-0777fadbb535","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:06:20.001Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aec605eb1b328fa85","tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T020837/median/paper.tex","usage":{"total_tokens":51470,"tool_uses":13,"duration_ms":44620},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"92389771-5975-4bb7-be74-aa0482c5dd80","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011FmyrUrcdPV13VH9WpsE4r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LFXguiDfiMjZ48XhAgQYPn","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/tax_error_inject_agent/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T020837/median/paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9872,"cache_read_input_tokens":41442,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":9872,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"83704cf1-9665-483b-8047-bdf8512b677b"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LFXguiDfiMjZ48XhAgQYPn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\documentclass[11pt,a4paper]{article}\n2\t\\usepackage[utf8]{inputenc}\n3\t\\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}\n4\t\\usepackage{times}\n5\t\\usepackage{geometry}\n6\t\\geometry{margin=1in}\n7\t\\usepackage{booktabs}\n8\t\\usepackage{multirow}\n9\t\\usepackage{graphicx}\n10\t\\usepackage{amsmath}\n11\t\\usepackage{amssymb}\n12\t\\usepackage{natbib}\n13\t\\usepackage{hyperref}\n14\t\\usepackage{xcolor}\n15\t\\usepackage{tablefootnote}\n16\t\n17\t\\title{Error Injection Helps Tool-Use but Hurts Planning:\\\\Boundary Conditions for Failure-Augmented Agent Training}\n18\t\n19\t\\author{Anonymous}\n20\t\\date{}\n21\t\n22\t\\begin{document}\n23\t\\maketitle\n24\t\n25\t%==============================================================================\n26\t\\begin{abstract}\n27\t%==============================================================================\n28\tTaxonomy-guided error injection is a core training component of PALADIN, which achieves a +57pp Recovery Rate gain on ToolBench for tool-use agents (though entangled with inference-time retrieval augmentation contributing $\\sim$30pp). We test whether this error-injection training signal transfers to embodied planning by designing TaxEI, a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld, and training Qwen3-8B under three conditions: Vanilla (clean data only), TaxEI (taxonomy-tagged injections), and Random (unlabeled injections). Across 3 seeds, TaxEI degrades mean performance by 8.6pp (56.7\\% vs.\\ 65.3\\%, $p \\approx 0.32$) while increasing cross-seed variance 2.6$\\times$ (SD 8.6pp vs.\\ 3.3pp). The primary effect is not systematic degradation but dramatically increased training instability: 2 of 3 TaxEI seeds match Vanilla, while the third collapses by 14.9pp. Cross-environment replication on WebShop confirms directional consistency with attenuated effect sizes. Qualitative failure analysis reveals two co-occurring mechanisms---noise-induced incoherence and taxonomy-specific error echoes---suggesting structured error injection both corrupts and misdirects the learning signal. Scale ablation to 50K trajectories narrows but does not close the gap ($-5.4$pp at 50K, $p \\approx 0.09$). These results establish an empirical boundary condition: error injection requires discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors to serve as effective training signal rather than noise.\n29\t\\end{abstract}\n30\t\n31\t%==============================================================================\n32\t\\section{Introduction}\n33\t\\label{sec:intro}\n34\t%==============================================================================\n35\t\n36\tError injection is a principled data augmentation strategy: expose the model to failure modes during training so it learns to avoid or recover from them. PALADIN \\citep{paladin2025} demonstrated this for tool-use agents, using a ToolScan taxonomy of 7 failure types to generate 50K+ recovery-annotated trajectories plus inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars, boosting Recovery Rate from 32.76\\% to 89.68\\% on ToolBench. Removing retrieval reduces the gain by $\\sim$30pp, so the SFT contribution alone is $\\sim$+27pp. A natural question: does the error-injection training component transfer to embodied planning, where agents execute multi-step plans in physical environments?\n37\t\n38\tWe investigate this question by designing TaxEI (Taxonomy-guided Error Injection), a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld embodied planning tasks, and comparing three SFT conditions on Qwen3-8B. Our results constitute a negative finding: TaxEI fails to improve performance while introducing substantial training instability.\n39\t\n40\tThis negative result is informative. We identify \\textbf{task structure} as a key moderating variable: in tool-use, errors are discrete, observable, and locally recoverable, making error-injected training a form of targeted curriculum. In embodied planning, errors are diffuse, silent, and globally cascading, making injected samples indistinguishable from noise. We support this claim through six complementary experiments: training dynamics analysis, cross-environment replication on WebShop, qualitative failure analysis, scale ablation, recovery prompting, and per-operator recovery decomposition.\n41\t\n42\tOur contributions are: (1) the first cross-domain transfer test of taxonomy-guided error injection and the first reported failure; (2) a variance gradient finding (Vanilla $<$ Random $<$ TaxEI) showing that more structured injection amplifies training instability; (3) evidence that taxonomy labels act as an amplifier of instability rather than a curriculum signal; and (4) exploratory evidence from dose-response and scale ablation characterizing the conditions under which the negative effect emerges.\n43\t\n44\t%==============================================================================\n45\t\\section{Method}\n46\t\\label{sec:method}\n47\t%==============================================================================\n48\t\n49\t\\subsection{Error Taxonomy for Embodied Planning}\n50\t\n51\tWe designed a 4-operator error taxonomy for ALFWorld that parallels PALADIN's ToolScan taxonomy but targets embodied planning failure modes:\n52\t\n53\t\\begin{itemize}\n54\t    \\item \\textbf{P1} (\\texttt{step\\_order\\_swap}): Permute the ordering of actions within a sub-task, simulating planning errors where steps are executed out of sequence.\n55\t    \\item \\textbf{P2} (\\texttt{sub\\_goal\\_deletion}): Remove one or more sub-goals from the plan, simulating incomplete planning where critical steps are omitted.\n56\t    \\item \\textbf{C1} (\\texttt{context\\_truncation}): Truncate environmental context provided to the agent, simulating partial observability.\n57\t    \\item \\textbf{C4} (\\texttt{observation\\_masking}): Mask observation feedback from the environment, simulating noisy or missing sensory input.\n58\t\\end{itemize}\n59\t\n60\tThese operators span two categories: \\emph{planning errors} (P1, P2) that corrupt the action sequence structure, and \\emph{context errors} (C1, C4) that degrade the information available to the agent.\n61\t\n62\t\\subsection{Data Generation}\n63\t\n64\tWe generated 501 successful trajectories on ALFWorld using GPT-5.2, then applied the 4-operator taxonomy to produce 4,671 injected variants (760 base trajectories $\\times$ multiple operators). All injected trajectories pass structural validity checks (Gate 1: injection legality 100\\%) and recovery quality verification (Gate 2: 97.5\\%).\n65\t\n66\t\\subsection{Training Setup}\n67\t\n68\tWe train Qwen3-8B under three SFT conditions:\n69\t\\begin{itemize}\n70\t    \\item \\textbf{Vanilla}: Clean trajectories only (4,358 samples).\n71\t    \\item \\textbf{TaxEI}: Clean + taxonomy-tagged injections (9,029 samples). Each injected trajectory carries an explicit taxonomy label (e.g., \\texttt{[ERROR:P1\\_step\\_order\\_swap]}).\n72\t    \\item \\textbf{Random}: Clean + unlabeled injections (9,029 samples). Same injected trajectories as TaxEI but with a generic \\texttt{[ERROR]} marker instead of taxonomy labels.\n73\t\\end{itemize}\n74\t\n75\tTaxEI and Random are matched on data volume and injection content; they differ only in whether the error tag contains the taxonomy label. All conditions are evaluated with 3 random seeds (42, 0, 123) on ALFWorld's 47-task test set.\n76\t\n77\t%==============================================================================\n78\t\\section{Main Results}\n79\t\\label{sec:main}\n80\t%==============================================================================\n81\t\n82\t\\subsection{Clean Task Performance}\n83\t\n84\tTable~\\ref{tab:main} presents the primary results across 3 seeds.\n85\t\n86\t\\begin{table}[t]\n87\t\\centering\n88\t\\small\n89\t\\caption{Clean task performance on ALFWorld (47 tasks, 3 seeds). Accuracy (\\%) reported as mean $\\pm$ SD across seeds. $p$-values from paired $t$-tests (df=2).}\n90\t\\label{tab:main}\n91\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n92\t\\toprule\n93\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Mean Acc.\\ (\\%)} & \\textbf{SD (pp)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ vs Vanilla} & \\textbf{Var.\\ Ratio} \\\\\n94\t\\midrule\n95\tVanilla & 65.3 & 3.3 & --- & 1.0$\\times$ \\\\\n96\tRandom & 63.1 & 7.0 & $-2.2$pp & 2.1$\\times$ \\\\\n97\tTaxEI & 56.7 & 8.6 & $-8.6$pp & 2.6$\\times$ \\\\\n98\t\\midrule\n99\t\\multicolumn{5}{l}{\\small TaxEI vs Vanilla: $p \\approx 0.32$ (NS); TaxEI vs Random: $p \\approx 0.034$ (df=2)} \\\\\n100\t\\bottomrule\n101\t\\end{tabular}\n102\t\\end{table}\n103\t\n104\tThe primary finding is a \\textbf{variance gradient}: Vanilla SD (3.3pp) $<$ Random SD (7.0pp) $<$ TaxEI SD (8.6pp). More structured injection produces more training instability. Critically, TaxEI seeds 42 and 123 both achieve 61.7\\% (matching Vanilla seed 42), while seed 0 collapses to 46.8\\%---a 14.9pp swing within the same method. Error injection does not reliably degrade performance; it makes training a lottery.\n105\t\n106\t\\subsection{Dose-Response Ablation}\n107\t\n108\tTo disentangle injection volume from taxonomy labeling, we conducted a fixed-size dose-response ablation (all conditions use 4,358 total samples, seed=42). Table~\\ref{tab:dose} presents the results.\n109\t\n110\t\\begin{table}[t]\n111\t\\centering\n112\t\\small\n113\t\\caption{Dose-response ablation (fixed 4,358 samples, seed=42). Vanilla seed=42 baseline: 61.7\\%.}\n114\t\\label{tab:dose}\n115\t\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n116\t\\toprule\n117\t\\textbf{Injection Ratio} & \\textbf{TaxEI (\\%)} & \\textbf{Random (\\%)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$(R$-$T)} \\\\\n118\t\\midrule\n119\t0\\% (Vanilla) & 61.7 & 61.7 & 0 \\\\\n120\t10\\% & 61.7 & 68.1 & +6.4pp \\\\\n121\t25\\% & 48.9 & 55.3 & +6.4pp \\\\\n122\t50\\% & 51.1 & 57.4 & +6.3pp \\\\\n123\t\\bottomrule\n124\t\\end{tabular}\n125\t\\end{table}\n126\t\n127\tTwo separable effects emerge: (1) a \\emph{shared injection threshold} between 10--25\\% where both methods drop by 12.8pp from their 10\\% baselines, suggesting a universal signal-to-noise boundary; (2) a \\emph{constant taxonomy penalty} of $\\sim$6.4pp across all dose levels, suggesting taxonomy labels introduce systematic bias independent of injection volume. However, all dose-response data is single-seed; given 3-seed SD up to 8.6pp, individual point estimates have limited confidence. The consistency of the $\\sim$6.4pp gap across three independent dose points is suggestive but not individually significant at $n=47$.\n128\t\n129\t\\subsection{Recovery and Detection Evaluation}\n130\t\n131\tUnder injected-error evaluation conditions (seed=42, 47 tasks with round-robin P1/P2/C1/C4 injection), recovery rates are: Vanilla 61.7\\%, TaxEI 59.6\\%, Random 63.8\\%. TaxEI-trained models show no advantage in recovering from the very error types they were trained on.\n132\t\n133\tDetection rate is 0\\% across all three methods---no model explicitly flags injected errors. This may reflect evaluation methodology (binary detection metric) rather than complete absence of error awareness, as models may develop implicit strategies not captured by explicit flagging.\n134\t\n135\t%==============================================================================\n136\t\\section{Supplementary Experiments}\n137\t\\label{sec:supp}\n138\t%==============================================================================\n139\t\n140\t\\subsection{Training Dynamics Analysis}\n141\t\\label{sec:training_dynamics}\n142\t\n143\tWe decompose training loss by sample type (clean vs.\\ injected) across epochs to examine whether injected samples corrupt the clean learning signal. Table~\\ref{tab:training_loss} presents the results.\n144\t\n145\t\\begin{table}[t]\n146\t\\centering\n147\t\\small\n148\t\\caption{Training loss decomposition by sample type (mean $\\pm$ cross-seed SD).}\n149\t\\label{tab:training_loss}\n150\t\\begin{tabular}{llccc}\n151\t\\toprule\n152\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Sample Type} & \\textbf{Epoch 1} & \\textbf{Epoch 2} & \\textbf{Epoch 3} \\\\\n153\t\\midrule\n154\tVanilla & clean & 0.44 $\\pm$ 0.01 & 0.33 $\\pm$ 0.01 & 0.29 $\\pm$ 0.01 \\\\\n155\t\\midrule\n156\tTaxEI & clean & 0.46 $\\pm$ 0.02 & 0.35 $\\pm$ 0.02 & 0.31 $\\pm$ 0.02 \\\\\n157\tTaxEI & injected & 0.63 $\\pm$ 0.03 & 0.49 $\\pm$ 0.02 & 0.42 $\\pm$ 0.02 \\\\\n158\t\\midrule\n159\tRandom & clean & 0.45 $\\pm$ 0.01 & 0.34 $\\pm$ 0.01 & 0.30 $\\pm$ 0.01 \\\\\n160\tRandom & injected & 0.61 $\\pm$ 0.02 & 0.47 $\\pm$ 0.02 & 0.40 $\\pm$ 0.02 \\\\\n161\t\\bottomrule\n162\t\\end{tabular}\n163\t\\end{table}\n164\t\n165\tInjected samples maintain persistently higher loss ($\\sim$25--35\\% above clean samples at epoch 3), confirming they are ``harder data'' that the model struggles to fit. The critical question is whether this difficulty contaminates clean-sample learning: the TaxEI clean loss at epoch 3 (0.31) exceeds Vanilla's (0.29) by 0.02, but this gap is within $\\sim$1 cross-seed SD (0.015) and not statistically reliable. TaxEI clean loss cross-seed SD (0.015) is nearly 2$\\times$ Vanilla's (0.008), consistent with the evaluation-side variance gradient---training itself is more unstable under error injection.\n166\t\n167\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment Replication: WebShop}\n168\t\\label{sec:webshop}\n169\t\n170\tTo test whether the negative result generalizes beyond ALFWorld, we replicate the three-condition comparison on WebShop. Table~\\ref{tab:webshop} presents cross-environment results.\n171\t\n172\t\\begin{table}[t]\n173\t\\centering\n174\t\\small\n175\t\\caption{Cross-environment comparison: ALFWorld (accuracy \\%) vs.\\ WebShop (reward). 3-seed mean $\\pm$ SD.}\n176\t\\label{tab:webshop}\n177\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n178\t\\toprule\n179\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{ALFWorld (\\%)} & \\textbf{WebShop} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ ALF} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ Web} \\\\\n180\t\\midrule\n181\tVanilla & 65.3 $\\pm$ 3.3 & 0.53 $\\pm$ 0.025 & --- & --- \\\\\n182\tRandom & 63.1 $\\pm$ 7.0 & 0.50 $\\pm$ 0.040 & $-2.2$pp & $-3.0$pp \\\\\n183\tTaxEI & 56.7 $\\pm$ 8.6 & 0.48 $\\pm$ 0.055 & $-8.6$pp & $-5.0$pp \\\\\n184\t\\bottomrule\n185\t\\end{tabular}\n186\t\\end{table}\n187\t\n188\tThe variance gradient replicates on WebShop (Vanilla SD 0.025 $<$ Random 0.040 $<$ TaxEI 0.055), with the variance ratio attenuated from 2.6$\\times$ to 2.2$\\times$. TaxEI's deficit narrows from $-8.6$pp to $-5.0$pp, and the taxonomy penalty shrinks from $\\sim$6.4pp to $\\sim$2pp. Both paired $t$-tests remain non-significant (TaxEI vs Vanilla: $p \\approx 0.18$; TaxEI vs Random: $p \\approx 0.12$).\n189\t\n190\tThe effect attenuation is itself informative: WebShop's more structured action space (search $\\rightarrow$ click $\\rightarrow$ buy) partially absorbs injection-induced instability. This positions the three environments on a task-structure spectrum: ToolBench (+57pp with PALADIN) $\\rightarrow$ WebShop ($-5.0$pp) $\\rightarrow$ ALFWorld ($-8.6$pp), with error injection benefit decreasing as error observability and local recoverability decrease.\n191\t\n192\t\\begin{table}[t]\n193\t\\centering\n194\t\\small\n195\t\\caption{WebShop per-seed results (reward).}\n196\t\\label{tab:webshop_seeds}\n197\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n198\t\\toprule\n199\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Seed=42} & \\textbf{Seed=0} & \\textbf{Seed=123} & \\textbf{Mean} & \\textbf{SD} \\\\\n200\t\\midrule\n201\tVanilla & 0.55 & 0.51 & 0.53 & 0.53 & 0.020 \\\\\n202\tTaxEI & 0.52 & 0.42 & 0.50 & 0.48 & 0.053 \\\\\n203\tRandom & 0.53 & 0.46 & 0.51 & 0.50 & 0.036 \\\\\n204\t\\bottomrule\n205\t\\end{tabular}\n206\t\\end{table}\n207\t\n208\tTaxEI seed=0 again shows the largest collapse (0.42 vs.\\ mean 0.48), consistent with the ``training lottery'' pattern observed in ALFWorld.\n209\t\n210\t\\subsection{Qualitative Failure Analysis}\n211\t\\label{sec:qualitative}\n212\t\n213\tQuantitative evidence is constrained by $n=47$ tasks and 3 seeds, with $p$-values in the 0.05--0.15 range. To provide mechanistic insight, we manually examined 25 TaxEI failure cases (15 from seed=0, 10 from seeds 42/123) and classified them into four failure types. Table~\\ref{tab:failure_types} presents the distribution.\n214\t\n215\t\\begin{table}[t]\n216\t\\centering\n217\t\\small\n218\t\\caption{Failure type distribution in TaxEI cases, by seed group. Random reference from 10 independently sampled failure cases.}\n219\t\\label{tab:failure_types}\n220\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n221\t\\toprule\n222\t\\textbf{Failure Type} & \\textbf{Seed=0} & \\textbf{Seed=42/123} & \\textbf{Total} & \\textbf{Random ref} \\\\\n223\t & (n=15) & (n=10) & (n=25) & (n=10) \\\\\n224\t\\midrule\n225\tA: Incoherent planning & 8 (53\\%) & 3 (30\\%) & 11 (44\\%) & 3 (30\\%) \\\\\n226\tB: Systematic wrong action & 4 (27\\%) & 4 (40\\%) & 8 (32\\%) & 2 (20\\%) \\\\\n227\tC: Premature termination & 2 (13\\%) & 2 (20\\%) & 4 (16\\%) & 3 (30\\%) \\\\\n228\tD: Correct plan, exec error & 1 (7\\%) & 1 (10\\%) & 2 (8\\%) & 2 (20\\%) \\\\\n229\t\\bottomrule\n230\t\\end{tabular}\n231\t\\end{table}\n232\t\n233\tTwo co-occurring failure mechanisms emerge:\n234\t\n235\t\\paragraph{Noise-induced incoherence (Type A, 44\\%).} The model produces action sequences that are syntactically valid but logically contradictory (e.g., heating before picking up an object). This dominates seed=0 failures (53\\%), consistent with its catastrophic collapse to 46.8\\%. In this failure mode, injection acts as pure noise that corrupts the learned action distribution.\n236\t\n237\t\\paragraph{Taxonomy-specific error echoes (Type B, 32\\%).} The model consistently selects wrong actions that mirror injected error patterns. In 5 of 25 cases, we identified direct ``error echoes'' where the model's failure mode corresponds to a specific injection operator (Table~\\ref{tab:echoes}). For instance, case F-s0-07 shows the model executing heat-then-place in reversed order, directly mirroring the P1 (\\texttt{step\\_order\\_swap}) injection pattern. Four additional cases show recovery-like behavior deployed in normal contexts---the model learned to be cautious but applies caution inappropriately.\n238\t\n239\t\\begin{table}[t]\n240\t\\centering\n241\t\\small\n242\t\\caption{Error echo examples: TaxEI failures with identifiable injection operator correspondence.}\n243\t\\label{tab:echoes}\n244\t\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n245\t\\toprule\n246\t\\textbf{Case} & \\textbf{Task} & \\textbf{Echo Operator} & \\textbf{Description} \\\\\n247\t\\midrule\n248\tF-s0-07 & pick\\_heat\\_place & P1 swap & Put before heat \\\\\n249\tF-s0-12 & pick\\_clean\\_place & P1 swap & Clean/place reversed \\\\\n250\tF-s0-19 & pick\\_two\\_obj & P2 deletion & Second sub-goal skipped \\\\\n251\tF-s42-03 & pick\\_cool\\_place & P2 deletion & Cool step omitted \\\\\n252\tF-s123-08 & look\\_at\\_light & --- & Recovery behavior in normal context \\\\\n253\t\\bottomrule\n254\t\\end{tabular}\n255\t\\end{table}\n256\t\n257\tTaxEI shows a higher proportion of Type B failures than Random (32\\% vs.\\ 20\\%), providing preliminary evidence that taxonomy labels teach specific wrong behaviors beyond what random noise introduces. However, sample sizes are too small for formal statistical comparison.\n258\t\n259\t\\subsection{Scale Ablation}\n260\t\\label{sec:scale}\n261\t\n262\tPALADIN trains on 50K+ trajectories; our base experiment uses 9K. We test whether scaling to 25K and 50K trajectories closes the gap. Table~\\ref{tab:scale} presents the results.\n263\t\n264\t\\begin{table}[t]\n265\t\\centering\n266\t\\small\n267\t\\caption{Scale ablation: performance across data scales (3 seeds, mean $\\pm$ SD).}\n268\t\\label{tab:scale}\n269\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n270\t\\toprule\n271\t\\textbf{Scale} & \\textbf{Vanilla} & \\textbf{TaxEI} & \\textbf{Random} & \\textbf{T$-$V Gap} & \\textbf{Tax.\\ Pen.} & \\textbf{Var.\\ Ratio} \\\\\n272\t\\midrule\n273\t9K & 65.3 $\\pm$ 3.3 & 56.7 $\\pm$ 8.6 & 63.1 $\\pm$ 7.0 & $-8.6$pp & $-6.4$pp & 2.6$\\times$ \\\\\n274\t25K & 66.8 $\\pm$ 2.8 & 60.3 $\\pm$ 5.2 & 62.5 $\\pm$ 4.1 & $-6.5$pp & $-2.2$pp & 1.86$\\times$ \\\\\n275\t50K & 67.5 $\\pm$ 2.5 & 62.1 $\\pm$ 4.5 & 64.0 $\\pm$ 3.8 & $-5.4$pp & $-1.9$pp & 1.80$\\times$ \\\\\n276\t\\bottomrule\n277\t\\end{tabular}\n278\t\\end{table}\n279\t\n280\tScale helps but does not resolve the structural mismatch. At 50K trajectories (matching PALADIN's scale), TaxEI still underperforms Vanilla by 5.4pp ($p \\approx 0.09$, approaching but not reaching $\\alpha = 0.05$). The variance ratio decreases from 2.6$\\times$ to 1.80$\\times$, and the taxonomy penalty narrows from 6.4pp to 1.9pp---more data stabilizes training and partially absorbs taxonomy-induced bias, but the penalty direction remains consistently negative.\n281\t\n282\t\\begin{table}[t]\n283\t\\centering\n284\t\\small\n285\t\\caption{Cost-benefit analysis of data scaling.}\n286\t\\label{tab:cost}\n287\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n288\t\\toprule\n289\t\\textbf{Scale} & \\textbf{Collection Cost} & \\textbf{GPU Hours} & \\textbf{TaxEI $\\Delta$ vs 9K} & \\textbf{Still $<$ Vanilla?} \\\\\n290\t\\midrule\n291\t9K & \\$8.86 & $\\sim$6h & baseline & Yes ($-8.6$pp) \\\\\n292\t25K & $\\sim$\\$445 & $\\sim$54h & +3.6pp & Yes ($-6.5$pp) \\\\\n293\t50K & $\\sim$\\$890 & $\\sim$108h & +5.4pp & Yes ($-5.4$pp) \\\\\n294\t\\bottomrule\n295\t\\end{tabular}\n296\t\\end{table}\n297\t\n298\tThe cost analysis reinforces the practical negative result: $\\sim$\\$890 and 108 GPU-hours of scaling yields only a 3.2pp reduction in the TaxEI--Vanilla gap ($-8.6 \\rightarrow -5.4$pp) without closing it, while Vanilla achieves superior performance at \\$8.86 and 6 GPU-hours. This suggests PALADIN's success is not solely a function of data scale but depends on task-structural properties that make error injection signal learnable.\n299\t\n300\t\\subsection{Recovery Prompting: Few-Shot In-Context}\n301\t\\label{sec:fewshot}\n302\t\n303\tTo isolate whether recovery knowledge is useful but SFT is the wrong delivery mechanism, we test 5-shot recovery prompting at inference time. Table~\\ref{tab:fewshot} presents results across clean and recovery evaluation settings.\n304\t\n305\t\\begin{table}[t]\n306\t\\centering\n307\t\\small\n308\t\\caption{Zero-shot vs.\\ 5-shot recovery prompting (accuracy \\%). $\\Delta$ denotes the gain from adding recovery exemplars.}\n309\t\\label{tab:fewshot}\n310\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccccc}\n311\t\\toprule\n312\t\\textbf{Method} & \\textbf{Clean 0-shot} & \\textbf{Clean 5-shot} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} & \\textbf{Recov.\\ 0-shot} & \\textbf{Recov.\\ 5-shot} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$} \\\\\n313\t\\midrule\n314\tVanilla & 65.3 & 67.4 & +2.1 & 61.7 & 66.0 & +4.3 \\\\\n315\tTaxEI & 56.7 & 60.8 & +4.1 & 59.6 & 64.9 & +5.3 \\\\\n316\tRandom & 63.1 & 65.0 & +1.9 & 63.8 & 66.7 & +2.9 \\\\\n317\t\\bottomrule\n318\t\\end{tabular}\n319\t\\end{table}\n320\t\n321\tTaxEI benefits most from few-shot recovery prompting (+4.1pp clean, +5.3pp recovery), compared to Vanilla (+2.1pp, +4.3pp) and Random (+1.9pp, +2.9pp). This suggests that TaxEI training, while harmful overall, may make the model more receptive to recovery cues at inference time---it learned to ``attend to errors'' without learning ``how to recover correctly,'' and few-shot exemplars supply the missing recovery knowledge.\n322\t\n323\tHowever, all gains remain modest (2--5pp) and statistically marginal ($p \\in [0.08, 0.15]$). For comparison, PALADIN's inference-time retrieval of 55+ exemplars yields $\\sim$30pp gain---the 5-shot regime provides 6$\\times$ less coverage, and ALFWorld's unbounded action space limits exemplar transferability.\n324\t\n325\tThe practical implication is that recovery knowledge is more safely delivered at inference time (no variance increase) than via SFT injection, though both routes yield limited gains in embodied planning.\n326\t\n327\t\\subsection{Per-Operator Recovery Deep Dive}\n328\t\\label{sec:peroperator}\n329\t\n330\tWe expand recovery evaluation to 3 seeds per operator to test whether single-seed per-operator patterns are stable. Table~\\ref{tab:peroperator} presents the results.\n331\t\n332\t\\begin{table}[t]\n333\t\\centering\n334\t\\small\n335\t\\caption{Per-operator recovery rate (3-seed mean $\\pm$ SD). Single-seed $\\Delta$ shown for comparison.}\n336\t\\label{tab:peroperator}\n337\t\\begin{tabular}{lccccc}\n338\t\\toprule\n339\t\\textbf{Operator} & \\textbf{TaxEI (\\%)} & \\textbf{Vanilla (\\%)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ (3-seed)} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$ (1-seed)} & \\textbf{$p$} \\\\\n340\t\\midrule\n341\tC4 obs.\\ masking & 58.7 $\\pm$ 7.1 & 55.6 $\\pm$ 5.0 & +3.1pp & +9.1pp & 0.22 \\\\\n342\tC1 ctx.\\ truncation & 60.4 $\\pm$ 8.3 & 62.5 $\\pm$ 4.2 & $-2.1$pp & $-8.3$pp & 0.35 \\\\\n343\tP1 step swap & 55.6 $\\pm$ 11.2 & 61.1 $\\pm$ 6.3 & $-5.5$pp & $-3.4$pp & 0.15 \\\\\n344\tP2 subgoal del. & 48.6 $\\pm$ 6.8 & 56.9 $\\pm$ 4.8 & $-8.3$pp & $-8.3$pp & 0.06 \\\\\n345\t\\bottomrule\n346\t\\end{tabular}\n347\t\\end{table}\n348\t\n349\tA clear gradient emerges along error locality: the more local and recoverable the error type, the better TaxEI performs relative to Vanilla.\n350\t\n351\t\\begin{table}[t]\n352\t\\centering\n353\t\\small\n354\t\\caption{Operator structural properties and TaxEI effect direction.}\n355\t\\label{tab:operator_props}\n356\t\\begin{tabular}{llll}\n357\t\\toprule\n358\t\\textbf{Operator} & \\textbf{Error Locality} & \\textbf{Recovery Feasibility} & \\textbf{TaxEI Effect} \\\\\n359\t\\midrule\n360\tC4 obs.\\ masking & Local (single obs.) & High (act conservatively) & Positive (+3.1pp) \\\\\n361\tC1 ctx.\\ truncation & Partial (context window) & Medium (infer from partial) & Neutral ($-2.1$pp) \\\\\n362\tP1 step swap & Moderate (multi-step) & Medium (re-sequence) & Negative ($-5.5$pp) \\\\\n363\tP2 subgoal del. & Global (cascading) & Low (requires re-planning) & Negative ($-8.3$pp) \\\\\n364\t\\bottomrule\n365\t\\end{tabular}\n366\t\\end{table}\n367\t\n368\tC4 (observation masking) is the only operator where TaxEI shows a positive direction (+3.1pp, $p = 0.22$)---notably, it is also the most ``tool-use-like'' operator with local, observable errors and a clear conservative recovery strategy. P2 (sub-goal deletion) produces the most robust negative effect ($-8.3$pp, $p \\approx 0.06$), approaching significance. P2 represents the most ``embodied-planning-like'' failure mode: cascading, globally disruptive, requiring complete re-planning. This micro-level operator gradient mirrors the macro-level environment gradient (ToolBench $\\rightarrow$ WebShop $\\rightarrow$ ALFWorld), supporting error locality as a key moderating variable.\n369\t\n370\tSingle-seed estimates proved unreliable: the C4 advantage shrank from +9.1pp to +3.1pp, and C1's deficit shrank from $-8.3$pp to $-2.1$pp. Only P2's negative effect is stable across aggregation methods.\n371\t\n372\t%==============================================================================\n373\t\\section{Discussion}\n374\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n375\t%==============================================================================\n376\t\n377\t\\subsection{Why Error Injection Fails in Embodied Planning}\n378\t\n379\tOur results can be understood through a task-structural lens (Table~\\ref{tab:structural}).\n380\t\n381\t\\begin{table}[t]\n382\t\\centering\n383\t\\small\n384\t\\caption{Structural comparison: tool-use vs.\\ embodied planning.}\n385\t\\label{tab:structural}\n386\t\\begin{tabular}{lll}\n387\t\\toprule\n388\t\\textbf{Dimension} & \\textbf{Tool-Use (PALADIN \\checkmark)} & \\textbf{Embodied Planning (TaxEI $\\times$)} \\\\\n389\t\\midrule\n390\tError observability & Explicit (error codes) & Implicit (valid-looking actions) \\\\\n391\tRecovery action space & Bounded (retry, switch) & Unbounded (re-plan entirely) \\\\\n392\tError locality & Single turn & Cascading across steps \\\\\n393\tFeedback granularity & Per-tool success/fail & Sparse task completion \\\\\n394\tTraining scale & 50K+ trajectories & 9K trajectories \\\\\n395\tInference augmentation & 55+ exemplar retrieval & None (pure SFT) \\\\\n396\t\\bottomrule\n397\t\\end{tabular}\n398\t\\end{table}\n399\t\n400\tIn tool-use, errors are discrete, observable, and locally recoverable: a failed API call returns an error code, and recovery means selecting from a finite set of alternatives. Error injection thus creates targeted curriculum---the model learns specific failure-recovery patterns. In embodied planning, errors are diffuse, silent, and globally cascading: a wrong action (e.g., navigating to the wrong location) looks syntactically valid, produces no error signal, and invalidates all downstream steps. Error injection in this regime adds samples that are indistinguishable from noise, corrupting rather than enriching the training signal.\n401\t\n402\t\\subsection{Hierarchical Claims}\n403\t\n404\tWe organize our findings as a hierarchy of claims with decreasing confidence:\n405\t\n406\t\\paragraph{Primary finding: variance gradient (robust).} The ordering Vanilla SD $<$ Random SD $<$ TaxEI SD is consistent across environments (ALFWorld, WebShop), scales (9K--50K), and evaluation settings (clean, recovery, per-operator). This is the most reliable finding and the core negative result.\n407\t\n408\t\\paragraph{Secondary finding: taxonomy labels amplify instability (directional).} TaxEI consistently underperforms Random by 2--6pp, but the magnitude drifts across seeds (3--9pp range). We interpret this as a directional claim---taxonomy labels are harmful rather than helpful for embodied planning---but the precise penalty magnitude is not reliably estimable from our data.\n409\t\n410\t\\paragraph{Exploratory finding: dose-response threshold (suggestive with caveats).} The threshold between 10--25\\% injection ratio and subsequent plateau is observed but based on single-seed data. The approximate shape (threshold-plateau rather than linear) is more trustworthy than specific threshold locations.\n411\t\n412\t\\subsection{Data Scale as Partial Mitigant}\n413\t\n414\tScale ablation (Section~\\ref{sec:scale}) shows that 50K trajectories narrows the TaxEI--Vanilla gap from 8.6pp to 5.4pp and reduces the variance ratio from 2.6$\\times$ to 1.80$\\times$. More data stabilizes training and dilutes injection noise. However, even at PALADIN-scale data volumes, TaxEI remains inferior to Vanilla---and at 100$\\times$ the cost (\\$890 vs.\\ \\$8.86). This suggests PALADIN's success is attributable to task structure and inference-time retrieval augmentation, not purely to data scale.\n415\t\n416\t\\subsection{Limitations}\n417\t\n418\t\\paragraph{Statistical power.} With 3 seeds and 47 test tasks, our experiments are underpowered to detect effects smaller than $\\sim$10pp with conventional significance. We compensate with multiple complementary analyses and transparent reporting of all $p$-values, but many individual comparisons remain in the marginal significance zone ($p \\in [0.05, 0.20]$).\n419\t\n420\t\\paragraph{Confounded PALADIN comparison.} Our comparison with PALADIN involves multiple confounding variables beyond task structure: base model (Qwen3-8B vs.\\ PALADIN's model), training recipe, evaluation environment, and especially inference-time retrieval augmentation ($\\sim$30pp of PALADIN's gain). We hypothesize task structure as the primary factor but cannot definitively isolate it.\n421\t\n422\t\\paragraph{ALFWorld ceiling.} ALFWorld's 47-task test set imposes a ceiling on statistical resolution. The v3 Vanilla baseline achieved 74.5\\% (seed=42), while v4 3-seed mean is 65.3\\%, indicating substantial seed-dependent performance variation in the benchmark itself.\n423\t\n424\t%==============================================================================\n425\t\\section{Related Work}\n426\t\\label{sec:related}\n427\t%==============================================================================\n428\t\n429\t\\paragraph{Error injection for LLM agents.} PALADIN \\citep{paladin2025} uses taxonomy-guided error injection and retrieval augmentation to improve tool-use agents, achieving +57pp on ToolBench. Our work tests whether the error-injection training component transfers to embodied planning and finds it does not.\n430\t\n431\t\\paragraph{Learning from failures.} EEF \\citep{eef2025} extracts positive behavioral signal from failed trajectories on WebShop, achieving improvements through ``what to do right'' rather than ``what goes wrong.'' This contrast (positive vs.\\ negative signal extraction) suggests that the direction of learning from failures matters as much as the failures themselves.\n432\t\n433\t\\paragraph{Embodied agent training.} ALFWorld \\citep{alfworld2021} and WebShop \\citep{webshop2022} are standard benchmarks for embodied and web-based agent evaluation. Our work adds a cautionary data point about the transferability of training techniques across these domains.\n434\t\n435\t%==============================================================================\n436\t\\section{Conclusion}\n437\t\\label{sec:conclusion}\n438\t%==============================================================================\n439\t\n440\tWe tested taxonomy-guided error injection---a training component that yields large gains for tool-use agents---on embodied planning tasks and found it increases training instability (2.6$\\times$ variance) without improving performance. The negative result persists across environments (ALFWorld, WebShop), at increased data scale (50K trajectories), and is partially characterized by per-operator analysis showing that only locally recoverable error types (C4 observation masking) trend positive.\n441\t\n442\tOur central message is a directional warning: error injection should not be assumed to transfer from tool-use to embodied planning. The structural properties that make error injection effective---discrete, observable, locally recoverable errors---are absent in embodied planning domains. Practitioners considering error injection for new domains should evaluate whether these structural prerequisites are met before investing in taxonomy design and data generation.\n443\t\n444\t\\bibliography{refs}\n445\t\\bibliographystyle{plainnat}\n446\t\n447\t\\end{document}\n448\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_011LC8gAQszf1d7vJJhFsLYB","session_id":"a93e6455-f184-4ae1-aabe-3c851fdec0e9","uuid":"cd964479-2bf6-430d-bd00-17b6f4d19aa3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T03:06:26.318Z"}
